so this started 10 years ago as a radio  play and I would like to understand  Charlie why you were a little reluctant  when you were approached to make it into  a movie well it was something we called  a sound play it was a staged radio play  and the idea was that there was nothing  on stage to look at other than the  actors reading there was a Foley artist  and there was Carter Burwell conducting  his musicians and the imagery was to be  created in the audience's mind that was  how it was written so that was how it  was written that was was what it was  written to be so when when I was  approached to do it I mean I wasn't  against it I just thought we'd have to  throw out that element which I quite  liked well we had been working at a  studio called starburns industries where  we produced animated television content  for shows on Adult Swim and another  example is community on NBC and and we'd  worked in this sort of comedy realm and  we were looking for opportunities to  broaden the horizon of stop-motion being  able to sort of take the the medium of  stop-motion and explore more adult  stories and and when we got an  opportunity to when I got an opportunity  to read this script it seemed I was a  huge fan of Charlie's as well and it was  just a beautiful script and it seemed  like a great opportunity to explore that  but it wasn't easy to get funding for  this this is really an independent film  and Paramount picked it up out of  Telluride in Toronto and really was  excited about it but that's that took a  while to get to that place explain some  of the reasons why this sort of breaks a  lot of the rules of what animation is  supposed to be we we tried to get  traditional financing we went to a  couple different places and it just  didn't work out for us so we turned to  Kickstarter we we did a 60-day campaign  and Duke and I had gone through the  studio when we found  that's from previous shows and old  puppets and we cobbled it all together  and made this three-minute video just  talking about what we really wanted to  do with this and 60 days later we raised  four hundred and six thousand that's  very good on Kickstarter so that was  enough to get you started  all right so explain a little bit about  the animation and Charlie you know how  you directed this together and how many  stages there were how many animators  there were I loved I loved going to  visit these places and seeing all the  little black boxes my role as the  director along with Duke was to create  the movie so you know we worked on the  design of the puppets we worked on the  design of the sets we we did an animatic  we recorded the actors you use the same  actors use the same actors we recorded  them and together which is an unusual  thing for animation you know we got the  production going a lot animation is very  front loaded because especially a  low-budget animation like ours because  we didn't you can't make you have to  know what going in you know down to the  frame what it is you're going to shoot  you don't have retakes and you can't  really make mistakes so you do the  animatic which is kind of basically the  movie the storyboards put to the dialog  in real time so you understand what the  movie is going to look like and then you  you you shoot it that's what we did we  had like a like 18 stages we had about  13 animators 18 stages by the end of  production we had eight identical michel  hotel rooms so on each of the different  stages they could be working on  different sequences and the directors  worked with everybody they would go and  they do their rounds about twice a day  one at 11:00 one at 3:00 and they check  in about the shot you know they would  move like for free you did like two  seconds a day or something I was  progressing very slowly they had a goal  of two seconds a day for animator her  I mean they rarely hit that so we  produced about a minute of footage a  week so I have written down the audience  so so what is this 3d printing how did  that help you in this what was that what  was explaining the mechanics of that to  me well when we recorded the actors they  delivered a very subtle small nuanced  vocal performance and so when we were  exploring the creative development of  the puppets we knew early on that we  wanted them to have the greatest range  of facial expressions so we focused on  the eyes is one fact one thing that we  spent a lot of time focusing on but the  other thing is the face and and how it  moves and so we settled on this type of  animation called replacement animation  where the face is split across the eyes  and you have brow pieces and mouth  pieces the mouth pieces are for all the  different phenome and the brow pieces  are for the different expressions of you  know that gives the emotion like the  furrowed and worried and surprised  there's about a hundred and fifty  different top pieces and about a hundred  and fifty different bottom pieces and it  gives the animators millions of possible  combinations to place them and create  the performance okay all right that  answers the question but you also chose  to keep the visible line why did you why  did you guys choose to do that once we  decided to use replacement animation we  didn't want to hide what it is from the  audience we wanted it to be understood  that this was stop-motion animation and  when that type of animation is is used  it's always painted out in post so you  can't see the seams and then you don't  really know what you're looking at they  do a lot of things to polish and remove  things like chatter and the clothing in  the air we kept that there's color  variations in the 3d printed faces which  we  kept and it it gives it a feeling of  being handmade and it gives it a feeling  it gives the characters a feeling of  being manipulated by outside forces and  which they are and it gives it them a  feeling of brokenness and well the other  thing that strikes me is you've just  taken us so much closer to a certain  kind of naturalism than most animation I  don't think I've ever seen any animation  that got this close and the conventional  wisdom is that when you do that you get  to some kind of uncanny valley  they were like or something that makes  people uncomfortable were you worried  about that we were certainly aware of it  it was we had lots of conversations  about it and one thing that we thought  would really help us is is the eyes you  know the eyes is literally being windows  to the soul so to speak so we wanted to  put a lot of attention to make sure that  the eyes felt alive each one of those  eyes you know Michaels 12 inches tall  and so his head is that big and the eyes  are you know like a like a like a little  bead so small in fact that we couldn't  find any like glass eyes that existed  for dolls or anything in the world so  they all had to be individually handmade  and hand-painted and they're moved by  hand individually so they have to stay  together you know it's very meticulous  in tedious and then also we made them  reflective so that they would not only  look moist but also so that they could  have eye lights which humans naturally  have if there's any light source present  humans have this reflective eye light  and so we the cinematographer Joe  Passarella it was like his mission to  make sure that these characters had I  lights and every scene so there were  these little grains of wheat or these  tiny little these tiny little bulbs  super tiny and they would be hidden in  the set in every direction so wherever  the character moved he would have an eye  light Wow that explains a great deal
 

